The requirements are: "without regressing tag extraction that already works today." Does `#world.` currently "work"? It returns `world.` — one could say that's part of the bug (punctuation being included in the tag name). The user's example `shopping list,#urgent.` should register `#urgent` — i.e., `urgent` without the trailing dot. So the intended semantics is: a tag is `#` followed by word characters (letters, digits, possibly underscores). The trailing `.` should not be included.

What about `#urgent.` — expected tag is `urgent`. And `(#ideas)` → `ideas`, so the leading `(` is not part of the tag (since the tag starts at `#`), and the trailing `)` should also not be included. So tag characters = word characters `[A-Za-z0-9_]` is a reasonable choice. What about hyphens, like `#hello-world`? Currently, the token `#hello-world` → `hello-world`. That "works today." If I restrict to `[A-Za-z0-9_]` only, then `#hello-world` would return `hello` — a regression! So I should include `-` in tag characters to preserve that behavior: `[A-Za-z0-9_-]`. Hmm, what else could appear in a token today? Any non-whitespace character. Examples: `#tag!` → today `tag!`; with `[A-Za-z0-9_-]` → `tag`. Is that a regression? The `!` case — the user's examples show that punctuation shouldn't be part of the tag. `!` is punctuation, so excluding it matches the intent. But strictly "what already works" — ambiguous. The safest interpretation that matches the reported bug: tags are `#` + an alphanumeric run, possibly containing underscores and hyphens.

